<BaseLayout title="About — Chris Plössl" description="An introduction to Chris Plössl, his engineering work, and the interests around it.">
  <section class="page-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
    <h1>Curious by nature.<br /><em>Engineer by profession.</em></h1>
    <p class="lede">I’m interested in how things work: software systems, the people who build them, and the ideas that shape the world around us.</p>
  </section>
  <article class="prose">
    <h2>A systems view</h2>
    <p>My professional path has crossed industrial automation, logistics and software engineering. Those fields have different tools, but all depend on understanding how parts fit together: technology, processes, constraints and people.</p>
    <p>That perspective still shapes how I approach engineering. Code matters, and so do the conditions around it: what a system needs to do, how it will be operated, and whether the people responsible for it can understand and change it.</p>
    <h2>Engineering with others</h2>
    <p>Alongside hands-on software work, I’m interested in technical leadership: creating shared context, making trade-offs clear and helping teams make sound decisions. Good leadership should make the work easier to understand and the people doing it more capable.</p>
    <h2>Outside software</h2>
    <p>My curiosity doesn’t stop at technology. I enjoy following ideas across science, history, philosophy and culture, and noticing where one field can offer a useful perspective on another.</p>
    <p>This site brings those interests together. It’s a place to describe the work in broad, public-safe terms and to share writing as it takes shape.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
